110 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 226 0 R
/E (Description: During passive HTTP inspection, it was discovered that the web server includes the Server header in its HTTP responses, specifically broadcasting the exact software and OS version: Apache/2.4.7 \(Ubuntu\).)
/Pg 15 0 R
/K [2]
>>
endobj
111 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 227 0 R
/E (Business Impact: Broadcasting exact software versions provides attackers with a precise blueprint of the server's architecture. Instead of spending time probing for vulnerabilities, an attacker can simply search vulnerability databases for known exploits specific to Apache 2.4.7, significantly reducing the time and effort required to launch a successful attack.)
/Pg 15 0 R
/K [3]
>>
endobj
112 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 228 0 R
/E (Remediation: Configure the Apache web server to obscure or completely suppress the Server header. In Apache, this is done by setting ServerTokens Prod and ServerSignature Off in the configuration file.)
/Pg 15 0 R
/K [4]
>>
endobj
113 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H3
/P 65 0 R
/T (Finding 3: Insecure Cookie Configuration)
/E (Finding 3: Insecure Cookie Configuration)
/Pg 16 0 R
/K [0]
>>
endobj
114 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 229 0 R
/E <FEFF00530065007600650072006900740079003A0020D83DDFE00020004D0065006400690075006D>
/Pg 16 0 R
/K [1]
>>
endobj
115 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 230 0 R
/E (Description: The web application issues sensitive session cookies to the user's browser without utilizing the HttpOnly security flag.)
/Pg 16 0 R
/K [2]
>>
endobj
116 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 231 0 R
/E (Business Impact: The HttpOnly flag prevents client-side scripts from accessing cookies. Without this flag, if a hacker successfully finds a Cross-Site Scripting \(XSS\) vulnerability anywhere on the site, they can use malicious JavaScript to steal the user's cookies. This allows the attacker to fully hijack the user's logged-in session without needing their username or password.)
/Pg 16 0 R
/K [3]
>>
endobj
117 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 232 0 R
/E (Remediation: Modify the application framework or web server configuration to automatically append the HttpOnly and Secure attributes to all session cookies before they are sent to the client browser.)
/Pg 16 0 R
/K [4]
>>
endobj
118 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H3
/P 66 0 R
/T (Finding 4: Absence of Anti-CSRF Tokens)
/E (Finding 4: Absence of Anti-CSRF Tokens)
/Pg 17 0 R
/K [0]
>>
endobj
119 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 233 0 R
/E <FEFF00530065007600650072006900740079003A0020D83DDFE00020004D0065006400690075006D>
/Pg 17 0 R
/K [1]
>>
endobj
120 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 234 0 R
/E (Description: Automated passive scanning via OWASP ZAP identified that HTML forms within the application do not utilize Cross-Site Request Forgery \(CSRF\) protection tokens.)
/Pg 17 0 R
/K [2]
>>
endobj
121 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 235 0 R
/E (Business Impact: Without Anti-CSRF tokens, the application cannot distinguish between a legitimate user request and a forged request. An attacker could trick an authenticated user into clicking a link on a malicious third-party website, which silently submits a state-changing action \(like changing an account email or password\) on the vulnerable site on the user's behalf.)
/Pg 17 0 R
/K [3]
>>
endobj
122 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 236 0 R
/E (Remediation: Implement the synchronizer token pattern. Ensure that all state-changing requests \(POST, PUT, DELETE\) require a unique, cryptographically secure, and session-specific Anti-CSRF token that the server validates before processing the request.)
/Pg 17 0 R
/K [4]
>>
endobj
123 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H3
/P 67 0 R
/T (Finding 5: Missing HTTP Security Headers)
/E (Finding 5: Missing HTTP Security Headers)
/Pg 18 0 R
/K [0]
>>
endobj
124 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 237 0 R
/E <FEFF00530065007600650072006900740079003A0020D83DDFE10020004C006F0077>
/Pg 18 0 R
/K [1]
>>
endobj
125 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 238 0 R
/E (Description: The web application fails to implement modern HTTP security headers, notably X-Frame-Options, X-Content-Type-Options, and Content-Security-Policy \(CSP\).)
/Pg 18 0 R
/K [2]
>>
endobj
126 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 239 0 R
/E (Business Impact: Modern web browsers have built-in defenses against attacks, but they require the server to activate them via headers. Without X-Frame-Options, the website can be invisibly embedded inside a malicious site, facilitating "Clickjacking" attacks where users are tricked into clicking hidden buttons. The absence of a CSP makes the application much more vulnerable to XSS attacks.)
/Pg 18 0 R
/K [3]
>>
endobj
127 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 240 0 R
/E (Remediation: Update the web server configuration to include robust security headers. At a minimum, implement X-Frame-Options: DENY \(or SAMEORIGIN\) and X-Content-Type-Options: nosniff. It is also highly recommended to develop and implement a strict Content-Security-Policy.)
/Pg 18 0 R
/K [4]
>>
endobj
